<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>
    <!--
      STRAT SHARED-CORE PARITY HARNESS.

      `scripts/ninjatrader/shared/StratCore.cs` states its own contract:

          "PURE Strat math, zero NT8 dependencies (no Series, no Draw).
           This is the C# mirror of scripts/libs_py/the_strat/ ...
           Rule changes go here AND in Python together, never in only one side."

      That last sentence is a COMMENT, and a comment cannot fail. This project is
      what turns it into something that can. It compiles StratCore.cs and nothing
      else (if a NinjaScript type ever leaks into that file the build breaks here,
      which is itself the contract being enforced) and exposes the five pure
      functions over a CSV, so `scripts/parity/strat_core_parity.py` can run the
      SAME inputs through both languages and diff the outputs.

      This is drift detection at the RULE level, where drift originates. A
      trade-set comparison tells you two implementations diverged; this tells you
      WHICH rule diverged and on WHICH input, with no NT8 in the loop at all.

      net8.0 rather than net8.0-windows: nothing here touches Windows, and the
      narrower target is a second, weaker check that no platform dependency has
      crept into a file whose whole value is being platform-free.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>Vinay.Strat.Parity</RootNamespace>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <AssemblyName>StratCoreHarness</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="StratCoreHarness.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../../ninjatrader/shared/StratCore.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
